module std::fs;

// File primitives (Rust's std::fs shape: functions returning values; errors
// are simulation/compile failures).
// Runtime-provided, like std::rand. std ships the PRIMITIVE only: format
// loaders (hex images, CSV stimulus, ...) are parsing libraries, not std.
//
//   read<String>(path)     decodes UTF-8 into a `String` in a #[test]; for a
//   read<Char[N]>(path)    hardware/top initializer the compiler bakes the
//                          text in as fixed `Char[N]`.
//   read<integer>(path)    reads raw little-endian binary integers.
//   read<T[]>(path)        in a #[test], reads the whole file into a
//                          runtime-sized array of T (`integer` or a packed
//                          numeric of any width): ceil(T'length / 8) bytes
//                          per element, a trailing partial element is an
//                          error. 'length, checked indexing, `for`, `T[]`
//                          parameters, element writes and `xs.push(v)` work;
//                          assigning the array copies it. A character enum
//                          (`read<Logic[]>`, `read<Bit[]>`) reads as text,
//                          one symbol per element, whitespace skipped.
//   read<T>(path)          reads those integers and constructs T from each one,
//                          e.g. `let rom: unsigned[16][256] =
//                          read<unsigned[16]>("rom.bin");`. Each value consumes
//                          ceil(T'length / 8) bytes. Short data zero-fills; data
//                          beyond the scalar/array target is an error. Hardware
//                          reads occur during compilation; #[test] reads occur
//                          in the generated executable.
//   exists(path)           runtime path probe in a #[test] (never fails)
//
// A relative path is the source file's directory's. A #[test] path may be any
// string computed when the test runs (a string local, an element of a string
// array, a string read from a fixture); a hardware/top read happens at
// compile time and takes a literal.
